\title{Tailcat client}
\description{
A tailcat client connects to the \link{TailcatServer} identified by a secret tailcat address. It can only dial that server. Network startup happens on the first \verb{$dial()}, \verb{$ping()}, or \verb{$disco_ping()}.

Blocking methods have an \verb{_async()} counterpart that returns a \code{\link[promises:promise]{promises::promise()}}; see \link{TailcatServer} for how asynchronous results are delivered, for timeouts (including \code{timeout = 0}), and for the error classes.

The address is a secret; printing a client never shows it or the private key, and the client does not keep a copy of the address in R.

Once a client is closed, every method other than \verb{$close()}, \verb{$format()}, and \verb{$print()}, and every field other than \verb{$is_closed}, raises a \code{tailcat_closed} error; nothing is kept from before. A client restored from a saved session is closed, because handles do not survive serialization.

A server with an allowlist (\code{allowed_clients} or \code{TailcatServer$allow_client()}) does not answer clients that are not on it, so for such a client \verb{$dial()}, \verb{$ping()}, and \verb{$disco_ping()} fail with \code{tailcat_timeout} once their timeout runs out, rather than with a distinct error.
}
